    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe centralisation in `OwnerEmail` is good: `isDisposable` + `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` sit naturally alongside `isPresent`/`isValid`/`normalize`, and the class javadoc was updated to cover the new \"disposable\" notion. That part is clean and idiomatic.\n\nThe problem is on the rejection side \u2014 the change routes a disposable-domain rejection through `InvalidEmailException`, and that exception is the wrong unit for this:\n\n1. **`InvalidEmailException` is syntax-specific and now mislabels the new case.** Its javadoc still reads \"present but not a syntactically valid address\" and its message is hard-coded to `\"Email must be a syntactically valid address: \" + email`. A disposable address *is* syntactically valid, so a client that submits `user@mailinator.com` gets a 400 whose message claims the address is malformed \u2014 a misleading, incorrect message. The exception has quietly become a catch-all for \"any reason email is rejected,\" and its own documentation/message were left describing only the original reason.\n\n2. **Two consecutive `if` blocks throw the identical `InvalidEmailException(email)`** for two genuinely distinct conditions (bad syntax vs. blocklisted domain). This duplication is the visible symptom of (1): the distinct notions the `OwnerEmail` javadoc now carefully separates (\"valid\" vs \"disposable\") collapse back into one indistinguishable escalation.\n\nSuggested direction (matches the project's own pattern \u2014 a dedicated checked exception + escalation handler per failure mode, per AGENTS.md):\n- Add a `DisposableEmailException` (checked) with its own message, e.g. `\"Email domain is not accepted: \" + email`, and an escalation YAML under `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DisposableEmailException.yml` responding 400 (it can share a handler shape with the existing one, but should carry its own meaning).\n- Throw that from the `isDisposable` branch in `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, leaving `InvalidEmailException` to mean only \"syntactically invalid.\"\n- If you deliberately want both to share one exception, then rename/re-document `InvalidEmailException` and its message to describe the broader \"unacceptable email\" contract rather than leaving the syntax-only wording in place \u2014 but a distinct exception is the more idiomatic fit here.",
